    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d672">

                <pb facs="1078" n="942" xml:id="pg942"/>

                <head>No. 672.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Thompson to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Port au Prince Hayti</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-11-06"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 6, 1888</date>.
                        (Received November 21.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 219.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Yesterday morning, the 5th instant, I
                    received a cable dispatch via Mole St. Nicholas, which read as follows:</p>
                <p>Protest instantly against action of Haytian authorities; man-of-war ordered.</p>
                <p>In the evening the United’ States ship <hi rendition="#italic">Boston</hi>,
                    Captain Ramsey, came into the harbor. This morning we commenced a joint study of
                    the seizure of the steamer <hi rendition="#italic">Haytian Republic</hi> and the
                    schooner <hi rendition="#italic">William Jones</hi>. My previous dispatch will
                    explain the attitude I have taken in the two affairs.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John E. W.
                    Thompson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
